  {
   "id": "C_Q4txznAwAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2019-07-02T09:09:41Z",
   "subject": "Re: make predictions from lavaan fit?",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:verdana,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;display:inline!important;float:none\">Is lavaan compatible with the predict() function in R base? </span></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>No, lavaan stands for LAtent VAriable ANalysis.  Its predict() method is used for calculating factor-score estimates, which is meaningless in path models without latent variables.  The usual<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> predict() </font>methods for calculating expected values of observed outcomes requires knowing what the values of your predictors are, which is unavailable by definition if you have latent predictors.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:verdana,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;display:inline!important;float:none\">Is there anyway to do this machine learning predictions? </span></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Jarrett Byrnes wrote a predict() method customized for path models in lavaan.  You can download it here:</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"https://github.com/simsem/semTools/blob/07a2313937927e49185474f2113d9698eb315fb7/semTools/R/fitted_lavaan.R\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/simsem/semTools/blob/07a2313937927e49185474f2113d9698eb315fb7/semTools/R/fitted_lavaan.R&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423430971000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1YKuDugRXio3SIUpBF_oCz\">https://github.com/simsem/semTools/blob/07a2313937927e49185474f2113d9698eb315fb7/semTools/R/fitted_lavaan.R</a><br></div><div><br></div><div>But I can&#39;t speak as to how it will work.  Your model is quite simple, so I expect it will provide what you need.</div><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" rel=\"nofollow\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423430971000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0S3RPEIDq2s9ULY1ZY-fW1\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "_AT7YT3nAwAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2019-07-02T09:12:02Z",
   "subject": "Re: make predictions from lavaan fit?",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I expect it will provide what you need.</div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Actually, I take that back.  Your model has a categorical outcome, and you are requesting predictions on the &quot;response&quot; scale (i.e., probabilities).  I don&#39;t know if Jarrett&#39;s function provides that (it might, you can read through it).  But for simple models, if you understand the parameters of your model, you should be able to write a function to calculate the predicted probabilities you need from lavaan&#39;s parameters.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" rel=\"nofollow\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423430972000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3ofu-AvOtie9uqnmwJw_3v\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
